WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.140
A Node.js application often begins with a basic login session and a password reset link.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.680
Then it grows.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.420
Mobile clients need API tokens.

4
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.000
An identity provider adds OAuth and OpenID Connect flows.

5
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.580
Webhooks need verification.

6
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.760
Admins need invite links.

7
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:23.340
CI/CD systems need deployment credentials.

8
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.080
Services need machine-to-machine access.

9
00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:31.220
Before long, tokens become invisible keys to the application trust model.

10
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.900
Many tokens are bearer values.

11
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.080
Whoever can present the token may be treated as authorized for some action.

12
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:55.620
That means a copied access token, reset link, API key, session ID, or refresh token can become a path to account takeover, unauthorized API access, privilege abuse, data exposure, fraudulent workflow approval, or supply-chain impact.

13
00:00:55.720 --> 00:01:00.840
The token may look like a string, but operationally it may carry real authority.

14
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.320
Token security is a lifecycle, not one library call.

15
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:15.860
Generation matters, but so do storage, transport, validation, scope, expiration, revocation, logging, monitoring, and leak response.

16
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:26.920
A token can be generated strongly and still fail if it is logged, placed in a URL, accepted for the wrong audience, stored without revocation, or allowed to live forever after a role change.

17
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:34.640
The goal is to make token behavior intentional, limited, protected, observable, and recoverable.

18
00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:46.660
Token systems should define what the token is for, who issues it, who validates it, where it travels, what authority it carries, when it expires, how it is revoked, and how the team responds if it leaks.

19
00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:52.760
That design discipline is what turns random strings into secure application controls.
